Let $E$ denote the given expression. Use $\sin x\cos x=\frac{1}{2}\sin(2x)$ first at $x=67.5^\circ$ and then at $x=135^\circ$. Each application introduces a factor of $\frac{1}{2}$.
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=2\,\sin\left(135^\circ\right)\cos\left(135^\circ\right)\\&=\sin\left(270^\circ\right)
\end{aligned}
$$
The terminal sine is $\sin\left(270^\circ\right)=-1$.
Therefore,
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\left(-1\right)\\&=-1.
\end{aligned}
$$

Let $E$ denote the given expression. Use $\sin x\cos x=\frac{1}{2}\sin(2x)$ first at $x=15^\circ$ and then at $x=30^\circ$. Each application introduces a factor of $\frac{1}{2}$.
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\sin\left(30^\circ\right)\cos\left(30^\circ\right)\\&=\frac{1}{2}\,\sin\left(60^\circ\right)
\end{aligned}
$$
The terminal sine is $\sin\left(60^\circ\right)=\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}$.
Therefore,
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\frac{1}{2}\,\left(\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)\\&=\frac{\sqrt{3}}{4}.
\end{aligned}
$$

